e,’ says Lydia."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["was"]},{"n":10},{"n":11},{"n":12},{"n":13},{"n":14},{"n":15},{"n":16},{"n":17},{"n":18},{"n":19}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2005-maj2-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2005-maj2-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read a short article. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (10-19) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"HERO OF THE MONTH","body":["Lydia Nash, the student who gave her £16,000 Who Wants to be a Millionaire? prize to charity ‘I didn’t want the Millionaire team to think I {{0}} some sort of do-gooder,’ says Lydia Nash. ‘But {{10}} was never any question that I wouldn’t give my winnings to the orphans I’d met during my gap year in Thailand.’","Oxford University student Lydia won £16,000 on the show in October 2003. But rather","{{11}} thoughts of paying off her student debts, {{12}} was the children of Pakkred, a tiny village outside Bangkok, who flashed into her mind when she heard Chris Tarrant’s familiar voice telling her {{13}} much she’d won.","She’d heard about the children’s suffering two years before, when she’d worked with Wasan Saenwian, a former Buddhist monk, {{14}} set up a foundation for children with disabilities in Thailand. His mission was to save the disabled orphans who {{15}} forced to live in horrendous, run-down orphanages. Thanks {{16}} the publicity Lydia generated and with the help of her donation, he can now finish building special centres","{{17}} the disabled children can get the medical support they need. Lydia went back to Thailand six months after her win. ‘The children were all playing football or watering the flowers. They were so grateful, {{18}} was fantastic. I’m not a saint. I think if anyone","{{19}} seen that poverty, they would have done the same,’ says Lydia."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["was"]},{"n":10,"accept":["there"]},{"n":11,"accept":["than"]},{"n":12,"accept":["it"]},{"n":13,"accept":["how"]},{"n":14,"accept":["who","to"]},{"n":15,"accept":["were"]},{"n":16,"accept":["to"]},{"n":17,"accept":["where","so"]},{"n":18,"accept":["it","which"]},{"n":19,"accept":["had"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2005-maj2-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr$2005-maj2-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 3, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$szóképzés (zárójeles szó)$mtr$, 8, 7, $mtr${"id":"2005-maj2-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about sea turtles. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (20-27).","Then write these words on the lines below in the appropriate form."],"title":"SEA TURTLE TROUBLE","body":["Coastal communities around the world are losing millions of tourist dollars a year through the {{0}} of rare sea turtles, a report claims. The World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF) says living turtles are three times more {{20}} than dead ones because of the tourism they attract.","Of the seven marine turtle species, three are critically {{21}} and a further three are in considerable danger. Sea turtles are killed for their meat and shells, with money also earned through the {{22}} of leather and eggs. The leatherback turtle, which can grow up to 2.75m long, has declined by 90 % over the last 20 years.","Campaigners hope the WWF {{23}} will encourage better conservation methods. The WWF report draws an {{24}} comparison between killing turtles or collecting eggs and generating money through tourism. After studying 18 sites in Africa, Asia, Latin America and the Caribbean, it says that marine turtle tourism is an investment in livelihoods and far more {{25}} both financially and for the future of ocean wildlife. Half the sites, where turtles were killed for money, produced an average annual income from sea turtle {{26}} of $582,000. The other half, where turtles have been a tourist {{27}}, had an income of $1.65m.","The study confirms what has been suspected all along; sea turtles are worth more to local communities alive than dead."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"destroy","example":true,"accept":["destruction"]},{"n":20,"prompt":"value"},{"n":21,"prompt":"danger"},{"n":22,"prompt":"sell"},{"n":23,"prompt":"find"},{"n":24,"prompt":"economy"},{"n":25,"prompt":"profit"},{"n":26,"prompt":"produce"},{"n":27,"prompt":"attract"}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
