ion, there’s no big strong man out there that is going to stop and help you refit your inner tube.”","Ironman is definitely for the fit, but not necessarily the young. Sister Madonna Buder, an American nun, completed the 2006 course in Hawaii aged 76, finishing with just over one hour to spare, double the world record of seven hours 50 minutes set in Germany in 1997."],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"coming first."},{"key":"B","text":"enter a triathlon."},{"key":"C","text":"London."},{"key":"D","text":"strength and stamina."},{"key":"E","text":"completing the course."},{"key":"F","text":"longer than other events."},{"key":"G","text":"mental and physical progress."},{"key":"H","text":"less than a day."},{"key":"I","text":"over a year."},{"key":"K","text":"mend their bikes."},{"key":"L","text":"varying physical conditions."}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"The Iron Maidens come from","answer":"C"},{"n":14,"stem":"To succeed, participants need","answer":"D"},{"n":15,"stem":"The Ironman competition takes","answer":"H"},{"n":16,"stem":"The regulations take no account of","answer":"L"},{"n":17,"stem":"The two women have been training for","answer":"I"},{"n":18,"stem":"Nicole finds training has resulted in","answer":"G"},{"n":19,"stem":"Completing the course is more important than","answer":"A"},{"n":20,"stem":"One thing participants might need to do is","answer":"K"},{"n":21,"stem":"Older people are capable of","answer":"E"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-R4$mtr$, $mtr$2012-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$reading$mtr$, 4, $mtr$bank$mtr$, $mtr$szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)$mtr$, 9, 4, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-R4","number":4,"kind":"bank","layout":"inline","examType":"szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)","points":9,"instructions":["Read this article about a tragic 19th century event. Some words have been removed from the text.","Your task is to fill the gaps (22-30) with the correct items from the list.","Remember that there are two more letters than you need."],"title":"DEATH IN THE VIENNA WOODS","body":["It was a scandal that shook an empire. At 7am on January 30th, 1889, the Archduke Rudolf, heir to the throne of the Austro-Hungarian Empire, was found dead together with his mistress, Baroness Marie Vetsera in the Imperial hunting lodge at Mayerling in the Vienna Woods, fifteen miles southwest of the capital. Was it suicide as {{0}} or was it murder? The police closed their investigations with surprising haste, and the dossier on the investigations {{22}} in the state archives as it should normally have been. Members of the Imperial household were sworn to secrecy. Yet, despite these attempts to cover up, almost at once {{23}} in Vienna of a politically motivated assassination.","The death of the Crown Prince had grave consequences for the history of Europe. Undoubtedly, it {{24}} in the developments that led to the assassination of the Archduke Franz Ferdinand by a Serb nationalist at Sarajevo in June 1914 and the subsequent drift into the First World War.","Unlike his conservative father, the Emperor Franz Joseph, Rudolf was reform-minded and liberal. His education {{25}} by one of his tutors, Father Jacint Rónay, who taught the young Rudolf about philosophy, the ethics of power and the history of the Austro-Hungarian monarchy. Rónay {{26}} in the Hungarian Democratic Revolution of 1848 to 1849; he had narrowly escaped capture and lived in England as an exile for almost two decades. With his writings and lectures during this period, he {{27}} as a radical philosopher.","Rónay was much impressed by the maturity of the young Rudolf and, as well as shaping his thinking in a liberal mould, instilled in him a deep sense of his historical responsibility. Using various pen names, Rudolf began to write newspaper articles and pamphlets {{28}} pan-German Austrian policies and spelling out his own radical ideas about internal reforms and foreign policies. By the end of 1888, Rudolf {{29}} the German Kaiser Wilhelm and the Emperor Franz Joseph, as well as powerful pan-German members of the Vienna establishment.","So were the rumours true? If so, who exactly were the paymasters who hired the hit-men for that January night in 1889? We may never know. What we do know is that Rudolf {{30}}: Wilhelm did try to create a Greater German Reich by force of arms and, in the process, totally destabilized the whole of Europe for the next half century."],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"openly criticising"},{"key":"B","text":"foresaw the future"},{"key":"C","text":"the official explanation claimed"},{"key":"D","text":"established an international reputation"},{"key":"E","text":"wild rumours began to circulate"},{"key":"F","text":"had been hugely influenced"},{"key":"G","text":"on opposite sides"},{"key":"H","text":"had been involved"},{"key":"I","text":"was a serious challenge"},{"key":"K","text":"had seriously upset"},{"key":"L","text":"was not deposited"},{"key":"M","text":"was a key event"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":22},{"n":23},{"n":24},{"n":25},{"n":26},{"n":27},{"n":28},{"n":29},{"n":30}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-R4$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-R4","number":4,"kind":"bank","layout":"inline","examType":"szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)","points":9,"instructions":["Read this article about a tragic 19th century event. Some words have been removed from the text.","Your task is to fill the gaps (22-30) with the correct items from the list.","Remember that there are two more letters than you need."],"title":"DEATH IN THE VIENNA WOODS","body":["It was a scandal that shook an empire. At 7am on January 30th, 1889, the Archduke Rudolf, heir to the throne of the Austro-Hungarian Empire, was found dead together with his mistress, Baroness Marie Vetsera in the Imperial hunting lodge at Mayerling in the Vienna Woods, fifteen miles southwest of the capital. Was it suicide as {{0}} or was it murder? The police closed their investigations with surprising haste, and the dossier on the investigations {{22}} in the state archives as it should normally have been. Members of the Imperial household were sworn to secrecy. Yet, despite these attempts to cover up, almost at once {{23}} in Vienna of a politically motivated assassination.","The death of the Crown Prince had grave consequences for the history of Europe. Undoubtedly, it {{24}} in the developments that led to the assassination of the Archduke Franz Ferdinand by a Serb nationalist at Sarajevo in June 1914 and the subsequent drift into the First World War.","Unlike his conservative father, the Emperor Franz Joseph, Rudolf was reform-minded and liberal. His education {{25}} by one of his tutors, Father Jacint Rónay, who taught the young Rudolf about philosophy, the ethics of power and the history of the Austro-Hungarian monarchy. Rónay {{26}} in the Hungarian Democratic Revolution of 1848 to 1849; he had narrowly escaped capture and lived in England as an exile for almost two decades. With his writings and lectures during this period, he {{27}} as a radical philosopher.","Rónay was much impressed by the maturity of the young Rudolf and, as well as shaping his thinking in a liberal mould, instilled in him a deep sense of his historical responsibility. Using various pen names, Rudolf began to write newspaper articles and pamphlets {{28}} pan-German Austrian policies and spelling out his own radical ideas about internal reforms and foreign policies. By the end of 1888, Rudolf {{29}} the German Kaiser Wilhelm and the Emperor Franz Joseph, as well as powerful pan-German members of the Vienna establishment.","So were the rumours true? If so, who exactly were the paymasters who hired the hit-men for that January night in 1889? We may never know. What we do know is that Rudolf {{30}}: Wilhelm did try to create a Greater German Reich by force of arms and, in the process, totally destabilized the whole of Europe for the next half century."],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"openly criticising"},{"key":"B","text":"foresaw the future"},{"key":"C","text":"the official explanation claimed"},{"key":"D","text":"established an international reputation"},{"key":"E","text":"wild rumours began to circulate"},{"key":"F","text":"had been hugely influenced"},{"key":"G","text":"on opposite sides"},{"key":"H","text":"had been involved"},{"key":"I","text":"was a serious challenge"},{"key":"K","text":"had seriously upset"},{"key":"L","text":"was not deposited"},{"key":"M","text":"was a key event"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":22,"answer":"L"},{"n":23,"answer":"E"},{"n":24,"answer":"M"},{"n":25,"answer":"F"},{"n":26,"answer":"H"},{"n":27,"answer":"D"},{"n":28,"answer":"A"},{"n":29,"answer":"K"},{"n":30,"answer":"B"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr$2012-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 1, $mtr$choice$mtr$, $mtr$feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)$mtr$, 8, 5, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"choice","layout":"inline","examType":"feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about an exciting expedition for young people. Some words are missing from the text.","Choose the most appropriate answer from the options (A-D) for each gap (1-8) in the text."],"title":"ALL ABOARD!","body":["Britney Bear, 20, from Atlanta, Georgia, cannot believe her good fortune. She is among twelve young people from seven countries {{0}} forward to an amazing adventure. On October 18, after ten days of intensive training in Switzerland, they will {{1}} off from Argentina for a two-week expedition. It will be the first of the twelve Pangaea (Pan Global Adventure for Environmental Action) Expedition trips.","The Pangaea Expedition will last four years. The twelve teams of different explorers will cross {{2}} of the oceans and visit the North and South Poles. Explorer Mike Horn will lead the teams. They will travel aboard Horn's Earth-friendly yacht, {{3}} built especially for these journeys. Their goal: To learn how {{4}} save the planet.","The first part of each expedition is {{5}} sightseeing. \"Once you've seen the beauty of the planet, you'll see that we have to conserve this beauty for the future,\" Horn says.","Next comes hard work. Cleanup projects rid the oceans and beaches {{6}} trash. Teams will use nets to skim the water. Then they will compact the trash into large plastic cubes that can be recycled. Coral reef programs will {{7}} young explorers to accompany professional divers under the ocean to reconstruct {{8}} coral reefs. Other teams will plant trees in tropical regions and reroute water in deserts. Each environmental project is overseen by experts in the field."],"items":[{"n":0,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"look"},{"key":"B","text":"is looking"},{"key":"C","text":"looking"},{"key":"D","text":"are looking"}],"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":1,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"put"},{"key":"B","text":"set"},{"key":"C","text":"get"},{"key":"D","text":"make"}]},{"n":2,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"every"},{"key":"B","text":"all"},{"key":"C","text":"the most"},{"key":"D","text":"whole"}]},{"n":3,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"what was"},{"key":"B","text":"that has"},{"key":"C","text":"that will be"},{"key":"D","text":"which was"}]},{"n":4,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"to help"},{"key":"B","text":"help to"},{"key":"C","text":"helping to"},{"key":"D","text":"to help with"}]},{"n":5,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"neglected by"},{"key":"B","text":"made up for"},{"key":"C","text":"devoted to"},{"key":"D","text":"included in"}]},{"n":6,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"out of"},{"key":"B","text":"from"},{"key":"C","text":"off"},{"key":"D","text":"of"}]},{"n":7,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"suggest"},{"key":"B","text":"make"},{"key":"C","text":"allow"},{"key":"D","text":"provide"}]},{"n":8,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"damaged"},{"key":"B","text":"abused"},{"key":"C","text":"injured"},{"key":"D","text":"wounded"}]}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"choice","layout":"inline","examType":"feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about an exciting expedition for young people. Some words are missing from the text.","Choose the most appropriate answer from the options (A-D) for each gap (1-8) in the text."],"title":"ALL ABOARD!","body":["Britney Bear, 20, from Atlanta, Georgia, cannot believe her good fortune. She is among twelve young people from seven countries {{0}} forward to an amazing adventure. On October 18, after ten days of intensive training in Switzerland, they will {{1}} off from Argentina for a two-week expedition. It will be the first of the twelve Pangaea (Pan Global Adventure for Environmental Action) Expedition trips.","The Pangaea Expedition will last four years. The twelve teams of different explorers will cross {{2}} of the oceans and visit the North and South Poles. Explorer Mike Horn will lead the teams. They will travel aboard Horn's Earth-friendly yacht, {{3}} built especially for these journeys. Their goal: To learn how {{4}} save the planet.","The first part of each expedition is {{5}} sightseeing. \"Once you've seen the beauty of the planet, you'll see that we have to conserve this beauty for the future,\" Horn says.","Next comes hard work. Cleanup projects rid the oceans and beaches {{6}} trash. Teams will use nets to skim the water. Then they will compact the trash into large plastic cubes that can be recycled. Coral reef programs will {{7}} young explorers to accompany professional divers under the ocean to reconstruct {{8}} coral reefs. Other teams will plant trees in tropical regions and reroute water in deserts. Each environmental project is overseen by experts in the field."],"items":[{"n":0,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"look"},{"key":"B","text":"is looking"},{"key":"C","text":"looking"},{"key":"D","text":"are looking"}],"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":1,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"put"},{"key":"B","text":"set"},{"key":"C","text":"get"},{"key":"D","text":"make"}],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"every"},{"key":"B","text":"all"},{"key":"C","text":"the most"},{"key":"D","text":"whole"}],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"what was"},{"key":"B","text":"that has"},{"key":"C","text":"that will be"},{"key":"D","text":"which was"}],"answer":"D"},{"n":4,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"to help"},{"key":"B","text":"help to"},{"key":"C","text":"helping to"},{"key":"D","text":"to help with"}],"answer":"A"},{"n":5,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"neglected by"},{"key":"B","text":"made up for"},{"key":"C","text":"devoted to"},{"key":"D","text":"included in"}],"answer":"C"},{"n":6,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"out of"},{"key":"B","text":"from"},{"key":"C","text":"off"},{"key":"D","text":"of"}],"answer":"D"},{"n":7,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"suggest"},{"key":"B","text":"make"},{"key":"C","text":"allow"},{"key":"D","text":"provide"}],"answer":"C"},{"n":8,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"damaged"},{"key":"B","text":"abused"},{"key":"C","text":"injured"},{"key":"D","text":"wounded"}],"answer":"A"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr$2012-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 2, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$nyílt cloze (egy szó)$mtr$, 10, 6, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about the ancient Olympic Games. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (9-18) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"ATHLETES AT THE ANCIENT OLYMPIC GAMES","body":["There were three main criteria for participation in the Games: the competitor had to be male, {{0}} Greek origin and a free man. The athletes trained individually over several months and travelled to a city close to Olympia, {{9}} a final selection was made and only the best {{10}} allowed to participate. They took an oath, and so {{11}} the judges. They promised to take part in the competitions in an honourable way, {{12}} breaking the rules.","For both training and competitions, athletes were always nude, to illustrate the ideal of harmony {{13}} the body and the mind. Without the protection of a layer of clothing, they had to take special care of their skin. {{14}} prepare for training, they covered their body with olive oil and then dusted it with fine sand. The oil and sand combination helped to regulate their body temperature and provided protection from the sun and from the stick the trainer used for beating them {{15}} they didn't perform the exercises correctly.","At the modern Olympic Games, the first, second and third-placed athletes are rewarded. In ancient times, there was only one winner, {{16}} prize was a wreath or crown of leaves. {{17}} Olympic champions did not receive any financial reward, they became important figures in their hometowns. They often took on a political role and they had the right to {{18}} a statue of themselves erected, and they could ask a poet to write verses telling of their achievements."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["of"]},{"n":9},{"n":10},{"n":11},{"n":12},{"n":13},{"n":14},{"n":15},{"n":16},{"n":17},{"n":18}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about the ancient Olympic Games. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (9-18) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"ATHLETES AT THE ANCIENT OLYMPIC GAMES","body":["There were three main criteria for participation in the Games: the competitor had to be male, {{0}} Greek origin and a free man. The athletes trained individually over several months and travelled to a city close to Olympia, {{9}} a final selection was made and only the best {{10}} allowed to participate. They took an oath, and so {{11}} the judges. They promised to take part in the competitions in an honourable way, {{12}} breaking the rules.","For both training and competitions, athletes were always nude, to illustrate the ideal of harmony {{13}} the body and the mind. Without the protection of a layer of clothing, they had to take special care of their skin. {{14}} prepare for training, they covered their body with olive oil and then dusted it with fine sand. The oil and sand combination helped to regulate their body temperature and provided protection from the sun and from the stick the trainer used for beating them {{15}} they didn't perform the exercises correctly.","At the modern Olympic Games, the first, second and third-placed athletes are rewarded. In ancient times, there was only one winner, {{16}} prize was a wreath or crown of leaves. {{17}} Olympic champions did not receive any financial reward, they became important figures in their hometowns. They often took on a political role and they had the right to {{18}} a statue of themselves erected, and they could ask a poet to write verses telling of their achievements."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["of"]},{"n":9,"accept":["where"]},{"n":10,"accept":["were"]},{"n":11,"accept":["did"]},{"n":12,"accept":["not","without","never","avoiding"]},{"n":13,"accept":["between","of","in","within"]},{"n":14,"accept":["to"]},{"n":15,"accept":["if","when","whenever"]},{"n":16,"accept":["whose"]},{"n":17,"accept":["Although","Though","While","Whilst","Whereas"]},{"n":18,"accept":["have","get"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2012-maj-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr$2012-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 3, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$szóképzés (zárójeles szó)$mtr$, 9, 7, $mtr${"id":"2012-maj-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":9,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about fingerprinting. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (19-27).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"FINGERPRINTS","body":["Everyone's fingerprints are different, and we leave marks on everything we touch. This makes fingerprints {{0}} for identifying individuals. People recognised the {{19}} of fingerprints 1,000 years ago. However, the FBI began collecting prints only in the 1920s. Ink and paper were used for taking fingerprints then. Today, people press their fingers on electronic sensors that scan their fingertips and create {{20}} images, which are stored on computers.","Despite the advances, fingerprinting is not an exact science. Prints left at a crime scene are often {{21}}. Also, our fingerprints are always changing slightly. Sometimes they're wet, sometimes dry. The process of taking a fingerprint can itself change the print. For example, the skin may roll, or the amount of {{22}} may vary.","Fingerprints are often used {{23}} to catch criminals. Unfortunately, however, authorities make as many as 1,000 {{24}} fingerprint matches each year in the United States.","Computer scientists all over the world are trying to develop computer systems for making {{25}} fingerprint matches. They have to be careful when writing programs to analyse prints. If a program requires too exact a match, it won't find any {{26}}. If it looks too broadly, it will produce too many choices. To keep these {{27}} in balance, programmers are constantly refining their techniques for sorting and matching patterns."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"use","example":true,"accept":["useful"]},{"n":19,"prompt":"unique"},{"n":20,"prompt":"digit"},{"n":21,"prompt":"complete"},{"n":22,"prompt":"press"},{"n":23,"prompt":"success"},{"n":24,"prompt":"correct"},{"n":25,"prompt":"accurate"},{"n":26,"prompt":"possible"},{"n":27,"prompt":"require"}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
