he last minute, it was always up against print deadlines, so there was never time to have great editorial debates about where exactly to cut. So we got the pyramid idea. If you write in this manner, then a designer can slap your copy on the page and, without even reading it, know where to cut. The ideal is to keep the complete story but they know they can simply chop off the last paragraph. And then the one before that. And the one before that. If they end up with just a headline and one single paragraph, the story still works. It was a straight technical requirement but the result is the core style of every printed newspaper you've ever read.","(UK Writing Magazine)"],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"a couple of years."},{"key":"B","text":"capital letters."},{"key":"C","text":"not about pyramids."},{"key":"D","text":"a newspaper."},{"key":"E","text":"the given length."},{"key":"F","text":"some basic questions."},{"key":"G","text":"an inverted triangle."},{"key":"H","text":"a practical problem."},{"key":"I","text":"the first part."},{"key":"K","text":"a long time."}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"In spite of the headline the article is","answer":"C"},{"n":10,"stem":"The structure of a news story can be likened to","answer":"G"},{"n":11,"stem":"In news stories key information is given in","answer":"I"},{"n":12,"stem":"The technique was invented to deal with","answer":"H"},{"n":13,"stem":"The computer term \"cut and paste\" goes back","answer":"K"},{"n":14,"stem":"Articles sent in rarely kept to","answer":"E"},{"n":15,"stem":"Scissors were often applied when putting together","answer":"D"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-R3$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$reading$mtr$, 3, $mtr$choice$mtr$, $mtr$igaz / hamis / nincs elég információ (A/B/C)$mtr$, 7, 3, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-R3","number":3,"kind":"choice","layout":"stems","examType":"igaz / hamis / nincs elég információ (A/B/C)","points":7,"instructions":["Read this article about a new invention and then read the statements (16-22) following it.","Your task is to decide whether the statements are true or false according to the text.","Mark a sentence A if it is true according to the article.","Mark it B if it is false.","Mark it C if there is not enough information in the text to decide if it is true or not."],"title":"SELF-CLEAN COATING COULD SPELL THE END OF WASHDAYS","body":["Cars that never need washing, clothes that repel stains and windows that clean themselves are just three potential applications for a revolutionary new \"paint\" that never gets dirty, scientists have said.","The self-cleaning coating can be applied to soft materials such as cloth and paper as well as hard surfaces made of glass or steel. It keeps on repelling dirt even after being rubbed with sandpaper or scratched with a knife.","Studies show that surfaces that have been coated with the titanium-dioxide paint become super-repellent to water and oil, with liquid forming spheres that simply roll away, carrying dust and dirt with them.","Clothing soaked in the water-repellent liquid effectively becomes waterproof and tests have shown that inky stains can simply be shaken off the fabrics.","\"Being waterproof allows materials to self-clean as water forms ball-shaped droplets that roll over the surface, acting like miniature vacuum-cleaners picking up dirt, viruses and bacteria along the way,\" said Yao Lu, a member of the research team at University College London.","Details of the self-cleaning coating were published last night in the journal Science by a team led by Professor Ivan Parkin, head of chemistry at UCL. He has specialised in self-cleaning technology based on surfaces that are super-hydrophobic – highly repellent to water. \"It makes surfaces so super-hydrophobic that it causes water to form marble-shaped droplets that roll away,\" Professor Parkin wrote.","The hydrophobic tendency of the titanium-dioxide paint is so strong that when the bottoms of toy boats were coated with it they floated higher in the water. If the same coating could be applied to the hulls of full-size ships they would need between 10 and 15 per cent less fuel, Professor Parkin said.","(The Independent)"],"options":[{"key":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"key":"B","text":"FALSE"},{"key":"C","text":"NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"The new \"paint\" is already widely used in the car industry.","answer":"B"},{"n":16,"stem":"The new coating can be applied to a range of materials."},{"n":17,"stem":"Rubbing or scraping the surface will remove the paint's special properties."},{"n":18,"stem":"Water runs off these surfaces, leaving them clean."},{"n":19,"stem":"Yao Lu said tiny vacuum cleaners were used in the research."},{"n":20,"stem":"A large team at UCL has been involved in the research."},{"n":21,"stem":"Professor Parkin talked to journalists about titanium-dioxide at UCL."},{"n":22,"stem":"The professor listed a number of potential industrial uses for the paint."}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-R3$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-R3","number":3,"kind":"choice","layout":"stems","examType":"igaz / hamis / nincs elég információ (A/B/C)","points":7,"instructions":["Read this article about a new invention and then read the statements (16-22) following it.","Your task is to decide whether the statements are true or false according to the text.","Mark a sentence A if it is true according to the article.","Mark it B if it is false.","Mark it C if there is not enough information in the text to decide if it is true or not."],"title":"SELF-CLEAN COATING COULD SPELL THE END OF WASHDAYS","body":["Cars that never need washing, clothes that repel stains and windows that clean themselves are just three potential applications for a revolutionary new \"paint\" that never gets dirty, scientists have said.","The self-cleaning coating can be applied to soft materials such as cloth and paper as well as hard surfaces made of glass or steel. It keeps on repelling dirt even after being rubbed with sandpaper or scratched with a knife.","Studies show that surfaces that have been coated with the titanium-dioxide paint become super-repellent to water and oil, with liquid forming spheres that simply roll away, carrying dust and dirt with them.","Clothing soaked in the water-repellent liquid effectively becomes waterproof and tests have shown that inky stains can simply be shaken off the fabrics.","\"Being waterproof allows materials to self-clean as water forms ball-shaped droplets that roll over the surface, acting like miniature vacuum-cleaners picking up dirt, viruses and bacteria along the way,\" said Yao Lu, a member of the research team at University College London.","Details of the self-cleaning coating were published last night in the journal Science by a team led by Professor Ivan Parkin, head of chemistry at UCL. He has specialised in self-cleaning technology based on surfaces that are super-hydrophobic – highly repellent to water. \"It makes surfaces so super-hydrophobic that it causes water to form marble-shaped droplets that roll away,\" Professor Parkin wrote.","The hydrophobic tendency of the titanium-dioxide paint is so strong that when the bottoms of toy boats were coated with it they floated higher in the water. If the same coating could be applied to the hulls of full-size ships they would need between 10 and 15 per cent less fuel, Professor Parkin said.","(The Independent)"],"options":[{"key":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"key":"B","text":"FALSE"},{"key":"C","text":"NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"The new \"paint\" is already widely used in the car industry.","answer":"B"},{"n":16,"stem":"The new coating can be applied to a range of materials.","answer":"A"},{"n":17,"stem":"Rubbing or scraping the surface will remove the paint's special properties.","answer":"B"},{"n":18,"stem":"Water runs off these surfaces, leaving them clean.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"stem":"Yao Lu said tiny vacuum cleaners were used in the research.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"stem":"A large team at UCL has been involved in the research.","answer":"C"},{"n":21,"stem":"Professor Parkin talked to journalists about titanium-dioxide at UCL.","answer":"C"},{"n":22,"stem":"The professor listed a number of potential industrial uses for the paint.","answer":"B"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-R4$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$reading$mtr$, 4, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$mondat vagy összefoglaló kiegészítése a szöveg alapján$mtr$, 6, 4, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-R4","number":4,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"mondat vagy összefoglaló kiegészítése a szöveg alapján","points":6,"instructions":["Read this article about an interesting English tradition and then read the gapped sentences.","Your task is to complete the sentences by filling the gaps (23-28) with one word only giving relevant information from the text.","Write your answers on the lines."],"title":"EGG THROWING CONTEST CLAIMS CENTURIES-OLD TRADITION","body":["\"Caution!!!!!! Flying Eggs!\" warns a sign on a field in the English village of Swaton, host on Sunday to the annual World Egg Throwing Championships.","Drawing hundreds of competitors and cheering spectators, it's a messy game claiming a 700-year history and a popularity that is illustrated by “egg-streme” puns.","Teams of two line up on the grass to try to throw and catch eggs without breaking the shell. Starting at 10 meters (11 yards) apart, the \"tosser\" throws an egg to a teammate, the \"catcher\". After each successful catch they spread further apart.","The winning team is the one that completes a catch over the furthest distance, with no breakage. This year it was Richard Gutsell and Michael Speakman.","\"It was tough ... We had a downward wind and that helped an awful lot, but it's mainly thanks to him because if you can't throw it that far you can't catch it,\" Speakman said of thrower Gutsell.","The World Egg Throwing Federation, set up in 2004, believes the game originated around 1322 when an abbot in the Lincolnshire village, the only person who had chickens, encouraged church attendance by giving locals a reward of one egg.","When the river flooded and prevented parishioners from attending Mass, monks are said to have thrown the eggs over to them, according to the Federation.","There are several disciplines at the contest, such as Egg Throwing, Russian Egg Roulette, Egg Static Relay, Egg Catapult and Egg Target Throwing.","In Russian Egg Roulette, which is a great favourite with spectators, two players sit facing each other, taking it in turn to pick from six eggs, one of which is raw, and smash them on their foreheads. Whoever avoids the uncooked one is declared winner.","\"At first people don't really know what to make of it ... but as they take part, listen in and see people's reactions they really buy in,\" event compere John Deptford said.","(reuters.com)","The World Egg Throwing Championships are held every {{0}}.","A number of funny plays on {{23}} are associated with this widely known contest.","As the competition progresses, the {{24}} between the teammates increases.","Winning the championship depends on only {{25}} basic criteria.","One of this year’s winners conceded that it was the {{26}} in the pair who was mainly responsible for their success.","It all started in the Middle Ages when people in the village received a(n) {{27}} in exchange for going to church.","In Russian Egg Roulette the loser is the person who picks the {{28}} egg."],"grading":{"spelling":"lenient"},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["year"]},{"n":23},{"n":24},{"n":25},{"n":26},{"n":27},{"n":28}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-R4$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-R4","number":4,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"mondat vagy összefoglaló kiegészítése a szöveg alapján","points":6,"instructions":["Read this article about an interesting English tradition and then read the gapped sentences.","Your task is to complete the sentences by filling the gaps (23-28) with one word only giving relevant information from the text.","Write your answers on the lines."],"title":"EGG THROWING CONTEST CLAIMS CENTURIES-OLD TRADITION","body":["\"Caution!!!!!! Flying Eggs!\" warns a sign on a field in the English village of Swaton, host on Sunday to the annual World Egg Throwing Championships.","Drawing hundreds of competitors and cheering spectators, it's a messy game claiming a 700-year history and a popularity that is illustrated by “egg-streme” puns.","Teams of two line up on the grass to try to throw and catch eggs without breaking the shell. Starting at 10 meters (11 yards) apart, the \"tosser\" throws an egg to a teammate, the \"catcher\". After each successful catch they spread further apart.","The winning team is the one that completes a catch over the furthest distance, with no breakage. This year it was Richard Gutsell and Michael Speakman.","\"It was tough ... We had a downward wind and that helped an awful lot, but it's mainly thanks to him because if you can't throw it that far you can't catch it,\" Speakman said of thrower Gutsell.","The World Egg Throwing Federation, set up in 2004, believes the game originated around 1322 when an abbot in the Lincolnshire village, the only person who had chickens, encouraged church attendance by giving locals a reward of one egg.","When the river flooded and prevented parishioners from attending Mass, monks are said to have thrown the eggs over to them, according to the Federation.","There are several disciplines at the contest, such as Egg Throwing, Russian Egg Roulette, Egg Static Relay, Egg Catapult and Egg Target Throwing.","In Russian Egg Roulette, which is a great favourite with spectators, two players sit facing each other, taking it in turn to pick from six eggs, one of which is raw, and smash them on their foreheads. Whoever avoids the uncooked one is declared winner.","\"At first people don't really know what to make of it ... but as they take part, listen in and see people's reactions they really buy in,\" event compere John Deptford said.","(reuters.com)","The World Egg Throwing Championships are held every {{0}}.","A number of funny plays on {{23}} are associated with this widely known contest.","As the competition progresses, the {{24}} between the teammates increases.","Winning the championship depends on only {{25}} basic criteria.","One of this year’s winners conceded that it was the {{26}} in the pair who was mainly responsible for their success.","It all started in the Middle Ages when people in the village received a(n) {{27}} in exchange for going to church.","In Russian Egg Roulette the loser is the person who picks the {{28}} egg."],"grading":{"spelling":"lenient","caseSensitive":false},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["year"]},{"n":23,"accept":["words"]},{"n":24,"accept":["distance","gap","space"]},{"n":25,"accept":["two"]},{"n":26,"accept":["thrower","tosser","other"]},{"n":27,"accept":["egg"]},{"n":28,"accept":["raw","uncooked"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 1, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$szóképzés (zárójeles szó)$mtr$, 8, 5, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about an exciting discovery. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (1-8).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"‘ARCHAEOLOGY ON STEROIDS’","body":["Archaeologists have discovered the {{0}} of a massive stone monument buried under more than one metre of earth only two miles from Stonehenge.","The {{1}} arrangement of up to 90 huge standing stones formed part of a C-shaped Neolithic arena that bordered a dry valley and faced directly towards the river Avon. The {{2}} used ground-penetrating radar to image about 30 intact stones measuring up to 4.5m tall. The fragments of 60 more buried stones reveal the {{3}} size of the monument.","“What we are starting to see is the largest {{4}} stone monument that has ever been discovered in Britain and {{5}} in Europe. This is archaeology on steroids,” said Vince Gaffney, an archaeologist at Bradford University, who leads the Stonehenge Landscape project.","The stones are thought to have been put into position more than 4,500 years ago to {{6}} a line around the southern edge of a natural depression. “We presume it to be a {{7}} monument, a ritual arena of some sort,” said Gaffney, whose team has mapped the ground and subsurface features around Stonehenge with state-of-the-art {{8}}. He will describe the latest findings from the site on Monday at the British Science festival in Bradford.","(http://www.theguardian.com)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"remain","example":true,"accept":["remains"]},{"n":1,"prompt":"hide"},{"n":2,"prompt":"research"},{"n":3,"prompt":"ordinary"},{"n":4,"prompt":"survive"},{"n":5,"prompt":"possible"},{"n":6,"prompt":"form"},{"n":7,"prompt":"religion"},{"n":8,"prompt":"equip"}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about an exciting discovery. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (1-8).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"‘ARCHAEOLOGY ON STEROIDS’","body":["Archaeologists have discovered the {{0}} of a massive stone monument buried under more than one metre of earth only two miles from Stonehenge.","The {{1}} arrangement of up to 90 huge standing stones formed part of a C-shaped Neolithic arena that bordered a dry valley and faced directly towards the river Avon. The {{2}} used ground-penetrating radar to image about 30 intact stones measuring up to 4.5m tall. The fragments of 60 more buried stones reveal the {{3}} size of the monument.","“What we are starting to see is the largest {{4}} stone monument that has ever been discovered in Britain and {{5}} in Europe. This is archaeology on steroids,” said Vince Gaffney, an archaeologist at Bradford University, who leads the Stonehenge Landscape project.","The stones are thought to have been put into position more than 4,500 years ago to {{6}} a line around the southern edge of a natural depression. “We presume it to be a {{7}} monument, a ritual arena of some sort,” said Gaffney, whose team has mapped the ground and subsurface features around Stonehenge with state-of-the-art {{8}}. He will describe the latest findings from the site on Monday at the British Science festival in Bradford.","(http://www.theguardian.com)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"remain","example":true,"accept":["remains"]},{"n":1,"prompt":"hide","accept":["hidden"]},{"n":2,"prompt":"research","accept":["researchers","research"]},{"n":3,"prompt":"ordinary","accept":["extraordinary"]},{"n":4,"prompt":"survive","accept":["surviving"]},{"n":5,"prompt":"possible","accept":["possibly"]},{"n":6,"prompt":"form","accept":["form"]},{"n":7,"prompt":"religion","accept":["religious"]},{"n":8,"prompt":"equip","accept":["equipment"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 2, $mtr$choice$mtr$, $mtr$feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)$mtr$, 9, 6, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"choice","layout":"inline","examType":"feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)","points":9,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about a newly revealed interest of the Queen. Some words are missing from the text.","Choose the most appropriate answer from the options (A-D) for each gap (9-17) in the text."],"title":"LOCH NESS MONSTER WAS NEARLY NAMED AFTER THE QUEEN","body":["The Loch Ness Monster could have been named after the Queen if it was ever found, amazing new documents {{0}}. A proposal to get ‘Nessie’ named after Her Majesty was sent to Buckingham Palace in the 1960s when a scientist discovered the Queen {{9}} with the legend.","Recently discovered papers show that Sir Peter Scott, the first person {{10}} an investigation into the existence of the mythical Scottish Highlands creature, asked the Queen {{11}} to have the monster named ‘Elizabeth Nessiae’ if it was ever eventually found. His {{12}} came after he found out Her Royal Highness was “very interested” in the existence of the prehistoric animal, The Independent reports.","Martin Charteris, who was the Queen’s private secretary {{13}}, said in a reply to Sir Peter’s letter that there would have to be “absolutely indisputable evidence” of its existence before it was named after the monarch. He said: “It {{14}} very regrettable to connect Her Majesty in any way with something which ultimately turned out to be a hoax. {{15}} the animal does prove to exist, I am not at all sure that it will be generally very {{16}} to name it after Her Majesty {{17}} it has for so many years been known as ‘The Monster’.”","(http://www.mirror.co.uk)"],"items":[{"n":0,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"are revealing"},{"key":"B","text":"were revealed"},{"key":"C","text":"reveal"},{"key":"D","text":"has revealed"}],"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":9,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"fascinated"},{"key":"B","text":"was fascinated"},{"key":"C","text":"was fascinating"},{"key":"D","text":"has been fascinated"}]},{"n":10,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"launched"},{"key":"B","text":"to be launched"},{"key":"C","text":"has launched"},{"key":"D","text":"to launch"}]},{"n":11,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"if she would agree"},{"key":"B","text":"that she agreed"},{"key":"C","text":"that she had agreed"},{"key":"D","text":"if she will agree"}]},{"n":12,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"recognition"},{"key":"B","text":"response"},{"key":"C","text":"requirement"},{"key":"D","text":"request"}]},{"n":13,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"at the time"},{"key":"B","text":"at the same time"},{"key":"C","text":"in the meantime"},{"key":"D","text":"at all times"}]},{"n":14,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"had to be"},{"key":"B","text":"must be"},{"key":"C","text":"would be"},{"key":"D","text":"is to be"}]},{"n":15,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"After all"},{"key":"B","text":"Even if"},{"key":"C","text":"Although"},{"key":"D","text":"Regardless"}]},{"n":16,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"improper"},{"key":"B","text":"fantastic"},{"key":"C","text":"sympathetic"},{"key":"D","text":"appropriate"}]},{"n":17,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"since"},{"key":"B","text":"so"},{"key":"C","text":"yet"},{"key":"D","text":"unless"}]}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"choice","layout":"inline","examType":"feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)","points":9,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about a newly revealed interest of the Queen. Some words are missing from the text.","Choose the most appropriate answer from the options (A-D) for each gap (9-17) in the text."],"title":"LOCH NESS MONSTER WAS NEARLY NAMED AFTER THE QUEEN","body":["The Loch Ness Monster could have been named after the Queen if it was ever found, amazing new documents {{0}}. A proposal to get ‘Nessie’ named after Her Majesty was sent to Buckingham Palace in the 1960s when a scientist discovered the Queen {{9}} with the legend.","Recently discovered papers show that Sir Peter Scott, the first person {{10}} an investigation into the existence of the mythical Scottish Highlands creature, asked the Queen {{11}} to have the monster named ‘Elizabeth Nessiae’ if it was ever eventually found. His {{12}} came after he found out Her Royal Highness was “very interested” in the existence of the prehistoric animal, The Independent reports.","Martin Charteris, who was the Queen’s private secretary {{13}}, said in a reply to Sir Peter’s letter that there would have to be “absolutely indisputable evidence” of its existence before it was named after the monarch. He said: “It {{14}} very regrettable to connect Her Majesty in any way with something which ultimately turned out to be a hoax. {{15}} the animal does prove to exist, I am not at all sure that it will be generally very {{16}} to name it after Her Majesty {{17}} it has for so many years been known as ‘The Monster’.”","(http://www.mirror.co.uk)"],"items":[{"n":0,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"are revealing"},{"key":"B","text":"were revealed"},{"key":"C","text":"reveal"},{"key":"D","text":"has revealed"}],"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":9,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"fascinated"},{"key":"B","text":"was fascinated"},{"key":"C","text":"was fascinating"},{"key":"D","text":"has been fascinated"}],"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"launched"},{"key":"B","text":"to be launched"},{"key":"C","text":"has launched"},{"key":"D","text":"to launch"}],"answer":"D"},{"n":11,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"if she would agree"},{"key":"B","text":"that she agreed"},{"key":"C","text":"that she had agreed"},{"key":"D","text":"if she will agree"}],"answer":"A"},{"n":12,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"recognition"},{"key":"B","text":"response"},{"key":"C","text":"requirement"},{"key":"D","text":"request"}],"answer":"D"},{"n":13,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"at the time"},{"key":"B","text":"at the same time"},{"key":"C","text":"in the meantime"},{"key":"D","text":"at all times"}],"answer":"A"},{"n":14,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"had to be"},{"key":"B","text":"must be"},{"key":"C","text":"would be"},{"key":"D","text":"is to be"}],"answer":"C"},{"n":15,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"After all"},{"key":"B","text":"Even if"},{"key":"C","text":"Although"},{"key":"D","text":"Regardless"}],"answer":"B"},{"n":16,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"improper"},{"key":"B","text":"fantastic"},{"key":"C","text":"sympathetic"},{"key":"D","text":"appropriate"}],"answer":"D"},{"n":17,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"since"},{"key":"B","text":"so"},{"key":"C","text":"yet"},{"key":"D","text":"unless"}],"answer":"A"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 3, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$nyílt cloze (egy szó)$mtr$, 7, 7, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":7,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about the danger of selfies. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (18-24) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"SELFIE MADNESS: TOO MANY DYING TO GET THE PICTURE","body":["The rise of selfie photography in some of the world’s most beautiful and dangerous places has resulted {{0}} a string of gruesome deaths worldwide.","The act of taking a picture of oneself with a mobile phone, placing the subject centre-stage, has exploded in popularity in recent years, with everyone {{18}} Britain’s Queen Elizabeth to President Barack Obama joining in.","But the selfie has also inspired an epidemic of risk taking and offensive public behaviour, pushing the boundaries of safety and decency, whether by dangling from a skyscraper {{19}} by posing with explosives.","Several governments have now begun treating the selfie {{20}} a serious threat to public safety, leading them to launch public education campaigns {{21}} to those against smoking and binge drinking.","Dozens of selfie-related deaths and injuries in early 2015 led Russia’s Interior Ministry to launch a campaign warning mobile phone snappers about the danger of, {{22}} other things, posing for a selfie with a lion.","“A cool selfie could cost you your life,” reads a poster from the campaign, {{23}} includes safety videos and information booklets.","The European Union has proposed a law to criminalise social media posts containing pictures of landmarks {{24}} as the Eiffel Tower in Paris or Rome’s Trevi Fountain.","(http://in.reuters.com)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["in"]},{"n":18},{"n":19},{"n":20},{"n":21},{"n":22},{"n":23},{"n":24}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":7,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about the danger of selfies. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (18-24) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"SELFIE MADNESS: TOO MANY DYING TO GET THE PICTURE","body":["The rise of selfie photography in some of the world’s most beautiful and dangerous places has resulted {{0}} a string of gruesome deaths worldwide.","The act of taking a picture of oneself with a mobile phone, placing the subject centre-stage, has exploded in popularity in recent years, with everyone {{18}} Britain’s Queen Elizabeth to President Barack Obama joining in.","But the selfie has also inspired an epidemic of risk taking and offensive public behaviour, pushing the boundaries of safety and decency, whether by dangling from a skyscraper {{19}} by posing with explosives.","Several governments have now begun treating the selfie {{20}} a serious threat to public safety, leading them to launch public education campaigns {{21}} to those against smoking and binge drinking.","Dozens of selfie-related deaths and injuries in early 2015 led Russia’s Interior Ministry to launch a campaign warning mobile phone snappers about the danger of, {{22}} other things, posing for a selfie with a lion.","“A cool selfie could cost you your life,” reads a poster from the campaign, {{23}} includes safety videos and information booklets.","The European Union has proposed a law to criminalise social media posts containing pictures of landmarks {{24}} as the Eiffel Tower in Paris or Rome’s Trevi Fountain.","(http://in.reuters.com)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["in"]},{"n":18,"accept":["from"]},{"n":19,"accept":["or"]},{"n":20,"accept":["as","like"]},{"n":21,"accept":["similar","comparable","corresponding","linked","connected","related"]},{"n":22,"accept":["among","amongst"]},{"n":23,"accept":["which"]},{"n":24,"accept":["such"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2017-maj-emelt-L4$mtr$, $mtr$2017-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 4, $mtr$lines$mtr$, $mtr$soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)$mtr$, 11, 8, $mtr${"id":"2017-maj-emelt-L4","number":4,"kind":"lines","layout":"lines","examType":"soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)","points":11,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about an unusual café. In most lines there is one word that should not be there. It is either grammatically incorrect or does not fit in with the sense of the text.","Read the text and then copy the extra word in the space provided after each line.","Some lines are correct. Indicate these lines with a tick ().","The task begins with two examples (0)."],"title":"THE REAL JUNK FOOD PROJECT","intro":"","items":[{"n":"0a","example":true,"text":"A “junk food” café, which serves on meals entirely from leftovers,","answer":"on"},{"n":"0b","example":true,"text":"has been set up by two women who are fed up with waste.","answer":null},{"n":25,"text":"Gaby Holmes and Natalie Crean, both of 23, are the driving"},{"n":26,"text":"force well behind The Real Junk Food Café, which has opened in"},{"n":27,"text":"Liverpool. They say it is shameful that just 35% of food ends up in"},{"n":28,"text":"the bin, at a time when no food banks are crying out for donations."},{"n":29,"text":"Waste food supplies are donated by greengrocers, caterers"},{"n":30,"text":"and small supermarkets. The two women decide it what they will"},{"n":31,"text":"cook on the basis out of what produce they’ve been given. People"},{"n":32,"text":"pay what they can for the food. The social concept café also"},{"n":33,"text":"accepts creative donations instead of cash in stock exchange for"},{"n":34,"text":"food. Gaby said: “It’s absurd so that millions of people are starving"},{"n":35,"text":"and millions of food items likely go to the bin every single day.”"}],"outro":"(http://www.mirror.co.uk)"}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
